; This file opens with a UTF-8 BOM — three bytes, EF BB BF, before
; the first character of the comment. `Lexer.init` skips them.
;
; That is not cosmetic. The two TypeScript hosts read a document through
; a UTF-8 decoder, which drops the BOM before their parser sees it, so a
; byte-oriented lexer that kept it made the reference reject a document
; that a shipped host accepted silently — three `unspecified` diagnostics
; on one side, none on the other. Skipping it is the only spelling under
; which all four hosts agree, and it is what every editor that writes a
; BOM without asking assumes.
;
; So this case asserts an absence: a BOM'd document validates exactly
; like the same bytes without one. Only at offset 0 — a BOM anywhere
; else is still three invalid bytes, which `Lexer.zig`'s unit test pins.
(plugin :name enc :version "1.0.0"
  (form :name a :open true
    (key :name b :type number)))

(a :b 1)
